1
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,910
Welcome back, everyone.

2
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:10,430
After we've been through
some more technical sessions

3
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:16,040
about Julia and Neuroblox
and how to code in both,

4
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:18,080
now we'll move more
into the neuroscience,

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:21,860
and we'll start with some
basic units of neuroscience,

6
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:26,780
talking about neurons, neural
mass models, and sources.

7
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,510
And these three
categories basically

8
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:33,390
are the outline for
this morning's lecture.

9
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:37,490
So first, we're going to go
through neurons, their biology,

10
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:40,880
and then how we represent
them in computational models.

11
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:43,400
Same for neural masses,
what assumptions we

12
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,520
make when we create
neural masses.

13
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:48,270
And then we'll
talk about sources.

14
00:00:48,270 --> 00:00:52,110
And for sources, we
have plenty of options.

15
00:00:52,110 --> 00:00:53,910
In Neuroblox, we have
some simple sources--

16
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:56,870
for instance, sinusoid
waves that you

17
00:00:56,870 --> 00:01:00,480
can input to any blocks
or some spike trains,

18
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,580
like Poisson series.

19
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:06,190
But for now, I'd like to focus
on the deep brain stimulation,

20
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:08,490
because that's one
category of sources that

21
00:01:08,490 --> 00:01:11,380
is very much a real
world application,

22
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:16,440
and we include this
already in Neuroblox.

23
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:21,700
So, moving on to neurons
first, as you might know,

24
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,000
our brain is full of them.

25
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,207
Human brains can have
around 10 to the 10 neurons.

26
00:01:28,207 --> 00:01:30,040
There's partially
connected with each other,

27
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:32,790
but still, dendrites of
most of these neurons

28
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:38,850
receive now on average an input
from 10 to the 5 other neurons.

29
00:01:38,850 --> 00:01:43,680
And also, neurons have a
soma and an axon coming out

30
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:44,850
of the soma.

31
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:47,970
The axon is how they
communicate the action potential

32
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:50,790
to the dendrites
of other neurons.

33
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:55,410
And here on the right, you
can see two images of neurons.

34
00:01:55,410 --> 00:01:58,470
On the far right, we have
some pyramidal neuron

35
00:01:58,470 --> 00:02:01,120
that has been actually
GFP-labeled You

36
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:02,150
can see in green.

37
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:05,230
And on the left, you
can see a drawing

38
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:09,370
that Ramon Cajal did of
Purkinje cells and granule cells

39
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:13,150
in the cerebral cortex.

40
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,620
So now, action potentials
are how neurons

41
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:19,010
communicate with each other.

42
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:21,370
These are chemical
signals that neurons

43
00:02:21,370 --> 00:02:24,110
turn into electrical ones.

44
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:26,320
And the way that
this is done is there

45
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:30,310
is some influx of
ions from outside

46
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:33,490
on the synaptic cleft
to the inside, which

47
00:02:33,490 --> 00:02:34,965
is the sum of the neuron.

48
00:02:34,965 --> 00:02:36,340
Eventually, the
sum of the neuron

49
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:37,790
starts from the dendrites.

50
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:42,940
And this influx happens
through receptors,

51
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:46,010
the gates that essentially
open at different times.

52
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:48,370
And the first
computational model

53
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:50,830
that actually described
these dynamics,

54
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:52,420
these receptor
dynamics in detail,

55
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:55,340
was the famous
Hodgkin-Huxley model.

56
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:59,000
And we'll see different
flavors of this model

57
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,090
implement it in Neuroblox.

58
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:04,070
And another success
of this model

59
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:05,750
is essentially
because of the way

60
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:09,200
that [INAUDIBLE] were
laid out, yes, it did

61
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,910
describe the receptor dynamics.

62
00:03:11,910 --> 00:03:13,980
But it also offered
us something else,

63
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:16,200
which is an equivalent
electrical circuit.

64
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,958
So this is another way of
thinking about the operation

65
00:03:19,958 --> 00:03:21,000
of single neurons, right?

66
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,260
So we can create an
electrical circuit

67
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:26,750
with actual
electrical components

68
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:30,690
like receptors and capacitors
and battery sources.

69
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:35,360
And a circuit like this
would behave in equivalence

70
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:37,350
to an actual neuron.

71
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:39,260
So this is a nice--

72
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:43,070
both abstract but also more
practical at the same time

73
00:03:43,070 --> 00:03:47,270
representation of this
computational model.

74
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,510
So in more detail, the plasty--

75
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:53,010
synaptic-- sorry--
transmission, as I said,

76
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:54,420
this is a chemical signal.

77
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:58,920
We have synaptic
vesicles being packaged

78
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:00,810
on the presynaptic
side, and then

79
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:02,800
they get emitted in
the synaptic cleft,

80
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:05,580
and they are picked
up by receptors

81
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:10,110
on the postsynaptic neuron,
which, consequently,

82
00:04:10,110 --> 00:04:14,350
the receptors actually
open the ion channels,

83
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:17,410
and we have the ion influx
that actually causes

84
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:21,390
the postsynaptic potential
because the influx actually

85
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:24,840
potentiates the neuron.

86
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,070
And people have
observed these two types

87
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:33,030
of neurons we call
integrators in class I

88
00:04:33,030 --> 00:04:34,780
and resonators, class II.

89
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:37,890
So we can distinguish them
by their fi-curve, which

90
00:04:37,890 --> 00:04:43,530
is the firing-rate frequency
over the injected current

91
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:44,260
to the neuron.

92
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,470
So we have neurons
that actually,

93
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:49,150
as you inject more
and more current,

94
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:54,210
the firing rate goes up
almost proportionally

95
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:55,480
to the injected current.

96
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:57,560
But also, we have
the resonators,

97
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:59,470
which, you can think of--

98
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:01,340
they function almost
like a switch.

99
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:05,690
So up to some specific input
current level, nothing happens.

100
00:05:05,690 --> 00:05:06,680
There's no firing.

101
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:08,620
And then there is a
jump to some firing

102
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:12,610
rate, which might grow
proportionally afterwards

103
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:14,500
for higher values.

104
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,030
And here on the
right, I just wanted

105
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:21,760
to show you essentially
some face portraits

106
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:23,210
of how this is possible.

107
00:05:23,210 --> 00:05:27,290
And this is coming from
a book by Izhikevich.

108
00:05:27,290 --> 00:05:29,770
This is the same
Izhikevich whose model you

109
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:31,570
have implemented, and
actually, that model

110
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:34,210
can exhibit these bifurcations.

111
00:05:34,210 --> 00:05:37,120
And by bifurcation,
essentially, we

112
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:39,880
mean different configurations
of the variables

113
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:43,060
and different evolutions of
the combination of variables

114
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:47,140
that allow for either
spiking or silence or moving

115
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:50,930
between rhythmic spiking to
silence or the other way around

116
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:56,200
or moving between bursts
to single spike and so on.

117
00:05:56,200 --> 00:06:00,950
And of course, to draw
diagrams like this

118
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:06,020
is much easier for simple neuron
models like the Izhikevich

119
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:07,680
neuron, which is
only two dimensional.

120
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:09,920
We can actually do this.

121
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:11,990
It's computationally
much easier compared

122
00:06:11,990 --> 00:06:14,300
to doing this for a
[INAUDIBLE] neuron, which

123
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:16,280
will have more equations.

124
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:19,910
So you need to actually
explore a larger parameter set

125
00:06:19,910 --> 00:06:22,470
and do this in a
simulation-based manner.

126
00:06:22,470 --> 00:06:26,480
So this is also a comment
towards the benefits

127
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:29,060
of simple neuronal models.

128
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:30,240
You have a simple model.

129
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:32,060
Maybe the parameters
and the variables

130
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:34,230
that it carries like
the Izhikevich neuron,

131
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:36,360
they are less
biologically relevant.

132
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:40,070
But you know more
about the behavior

133
00:06:40,070 --> 00:06:46,822
that model can exhibit more
easily, sometimes even in paper.

134
00:06:46,822 --> 00:06:50,100
Right, moving on to
the second category,

135
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:51,570
these are the
neural mass models.

136
00:06:51,570 --> 00:06:54,590
So you can think of neural
mass models as a step

137
00:06:54,590 --> 00:06:58,750
up on the abstraction ladder.

138
00:06:58,750 --> 00:07:00,760
Such models were
designed to capture,

139
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:05,590
on average, the activity
of neurons or neurons

140
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:06,890
connected to one another.

141
00:07:06,890 --> 00:07:12,190
And in general, they are
oscillators or collections

142
00:07:12,190 --> 00:07:13,100
of oscillators.

143
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:15,610
And here, you can see an
example of that, which

144
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:17,600
is the [INAUDIBLE] oscillator.

145
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,050
So these are very much
phenomenological models,

146
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:22,250
as in they don't
rely on biology.

147
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:25,960
They just imitate
this periodicity

148
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:28,760
in spiking, right, but
in very simple terms.

149
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:31,300
So they just assume
this sinusoid spiking,

150
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:34,570
and you have a pool of them.

151
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:37,160
But the handy thing about
the [INAUDIBLE] oscillators,

152
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:38,680
as you can see in
the diagram, is

153
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:41,420
that given a pool
of oscillators,

154
00:07:41,420 --> 00:07:44,900
you can, by changing this
one variable in the model,

155
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:49,960
you can move from regimes of all
of the oscillators oscillating

156
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:53,950
in a similar frequency so
now you have synchronicity

157
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:58,010
to regimes where oscillators
are more uniformly scattered,

158
00:07:58,010 --> 00:08:00,260
and there is no
synchronicity, or they're

159
00:08:00,260 --> 00:08:02,810
split into different clusters.

160
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:06,690
So you have some
flexibility in this model.

161
00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:09,860
Now, there are even more
detailed neural mass models

162
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:12,650
that allow for more
flexibility, and again, these

163
00:08:12,650 --> 00:08:15,810
are all examples that we have
already included in Neuroblox,

164
00:08:15,810 --> 00:08:20,960
like the Wilson-Coawn model,
which is a model that describes

165
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:24,210
an EI balance in a
neuron population,

166
00:08:24,210 --> 00:08:26,102
the Jensen rate
model that is, again,

167
00:08:26,102 --> 00:08:27,560
a model that
describes interactions

168
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:30,060
between pyramidal
neurons and interneurons,

169
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:32,750
or [INAUDIBLE], which
you can actually

170
00:08:32,750 --> 00:08:36,830
see a face portrait of
that here on the right.

171
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:39,150
And I'm showing you
the face portrait

172
00:08:39,150 --> 00:08:41,669
so you can see actually the
flexibility in this model.

173
00:08:41,669 --> 00:08:44,390
So each color here
is a different regime

174
00:08:44,390 --> 00:08:46,760
that the model can be in
depending on its parameter

175
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:47,550
values.

176
00:08:47,550 --> 00:08:50,360
So you can actually
tune it and observe

177
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:54,470
different behaviors in this
model according to your data

178
00:08:54,470 --> 00:08:55,170
as well.

179
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:57,780
So there's even more flexibility
to the simple [INAUDIBLE]

180
00:08:57,780 --> 00:08:59,950
oscillators from before.

181
00:08:59,950 --> 00:09:04,320
And for maximum
flexibility, I would say,

182
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:06,900
we have this next generation
neural mass models, again

183
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:08,680
already implemented
in Neuroblox.

184
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:13,500
So these models are maximally
flexible because they

185
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:17,230
are analytically-derived from a
population of neuronal models.

186
00:09:17,230 --> 00:09:22,300
So we first assume how
our neurons are behaving.

187
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:26,490
For instance, there have been
already analytically-derived

188
00:09:26,490 --> 00:09:29,070
in the literature
for QIF neurons.

189
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:30,870
That's the Quadratic
Integrated Firing

190
00:09:30,870 --> 00:09:33,450
neurons you have seen
in previous exercises

191
00:09:33,450 --> 00:09:36,550
or Izhikevich neurons that
you have implemented yourself.

192
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,480
So if you assume a
population of such neurons

193
00:09:39,480 --> 00:09:41,260
and some connectivity
between them,

194
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:43,920
you can analytically derive
a next generation neural mass

195
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:47,250
model that is more flexible
than the previous models

196
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:48,570
that I've shown you.

197
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:51,370
And you can see an example
of this flexibility here.

198
00:09:51,370 --> 00:09:55,970
For instance, this period
of synchronous activity,

199
00:09:55,970 --> 00:09:58,480
synchronous spiking
in our population,

200
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:01,780
is actually longer than
the quiescence period

201
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:03,860
and the other activity periods.

202
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:06,190
So you can even
tell from this image

203
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:11,720
that this model can do more than
simple periodic activity, right?

204
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:14,930
It doesn't assume sinusoid
neurons inside it.

205
00:10:14,930 --> 00:10:15,950
It's more flexible.

206
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:18,070
So it can capture
even transient periods

207
00:10:18,070 --> 00:10:20,440
like that or shorter
or longer periods

208
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,890
of activity and quiescence.

209
00:10:23,890 --> 00:10:28,660
So the final section that
I wanted to talk about

210
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:32,140
is our sources and specifically
deep brain stimulation.

211
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:35,780
So deep brain stimulation is
a neurosurgical procedure.

212
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:38,110
It has been applied--

213
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:40,120
I think one classic
application of it

214
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:45,070
is Parkinson's disease to reduce
motor symptoms like tremor

215
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:47,470
and rigidity.

216
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:50,840
The benefits of it is that it
is adjustable and reversible.

217
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:55,110
And also, it allows thus for
better medication management.

218
00:10:55,110 --> 00:10:56,840
But also, now it
has been extended,

219
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:02,960
and other brain disorders
can also be treated with DBS.

220
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:06,480
So as I was saying, this is very
much a real world application,

221
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:10,010
and we're trying to match
exactly those real world

222
00:11:10,010 --> 00:11:11,430
protocols in Neuroblox.

223
00:11:11,430 --> 00:11:14,120
So these are two
examples of stimuli

224
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:18,440
from the DBS suite of
stimuli that we have.

225
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:23,330
For instance, you can use
this DBS simple structure

226
00:11:23,330 --> 00:11:27,740
given all these parameters
with the relevant units which

227
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:31,230
will essentially create a
source that operates like this.

228
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:34,250
You have square
pulses interspersed

229
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:36,200
in some periodic manner.

230
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:38,660
And here, you can
see just a zoomed

231
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:40,640
in image of a squared pulse.

232
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:43,670
And you can have it with
smoothed corners or completely

233
00:11:43,670 --> 00:11:45,080
square.

234
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:47,070
But also, we offer
more flexibility.

235
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:49,460
So you can have these more
complex protocols where

236
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:54,120
you have a burst of pulses
and then a period of silence

237
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,390
and then a burst again.

238
00:11:55,390 --> 00:11:58,410
So this is the protocol DBS
over the previous structure

239
00:11:58,410 --> 00:12:01,620
that was just DBS.

240
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:05,100
And finally, I just want to
mention that we implemented

241
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:10,410
those structures, both of
DBS, and we replicated results

242
00:12:10,410 --> 00:12:13,080
from deep brain
stimulation paper

243
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:18,490
that essentially modeling
DBS in basal ganglia.

244
00:12:18,490 --> 00:12:21,300
And we have a model of
this, and these are just

245
00:12:21,300 --> 00:12:25,410
power spectrum results
from simulating

246
00:12:25,410 --> 00:12:29,190
the model in these three regimes
under different stimulation

247
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:30,210
conditions.

248
00:12:30,210 --> 00:12:33,960
And you can see more about
this in the exercises as well.

249
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:36,040
So this was my last slide.

250
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:39,000
So, similar to
previous days, you

251
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,190
can navigate to the
neural-- neuron masses

252
00:12:41,190 --> 00:12:43,200
and source [INAUDIBLE].

